 |\n| `HopperScaleLayout` | `Strided(lhs)` | 64 × 8192 × 256 | 4 | 0.659520\n| 0.096608 | 6.83× | 2.779 | 12.1% → 82.9% |\n| `HopperScaleLayout` | `Strided(lhs)` | 64 × 8192 × 256 | 8 | 0.659168\n| 0.099248 | 6.64× | 2.705 | 12.2% → 80.7% |\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Validation and changed files</summary>\n\n## Validation\n\nTriton 3.8.0; Blackwell on GB300 and Hopper on H100 80GB. Blackwell:\n1092 layout tests passed after merging current main. Hopper: 1373\npassed, 8 skipped. An additional 96 compile-warmup cases passed (64\nBlackwell, 32 Hopper), matching compiled kernel hashes and launch grids\nto real execution across both directions, matrix-major orders, strided\nstorage offsets, and optional output buffers. The 64 existing FP4\nallocation cases, 12 compact CPU axis-alias cases, and two runtime\nwarmup checks also passed. All 10 ordinary FakeTensor scale cases also\npass with an explicit no-launch guard; removing the FakeTensor dispatch\ncheck makes all six CUDA cases fail at that guard before launch. A\nfresh-process regression confirms that importing `triton_kernels.matmul`\nleaves CUDA uninitialized; it fails on `19d54baa2b` and passes here.\n\nThe benchmark tables use byte-checked runs of all 68 Blackwell and 48\nHopper API cases with per-sample L2 clearing, including allocated and\npreallocated outputs. All configurations in the 118-case Blackwell\ntuning sweep were also rerun and byte-checked. Doubling each eviction\nbuffer left the 6 MiB copy calibration within 1%. The dispatch cleanup\npreserved identical PTX and launch arguments in 49 GB300 and 84 H100\ncomparisons. Reusing the existing Hopper decoder was additionally\nchecked against `19d54baa2b`: all 48 API cases passed byte checks,\ngeometric-mean GPU time changed by +0.16%, and all 12 distinct PTX\nkernel bodies matched after removing debug metadata.\n\nA fresh regression comparison against the previous PR head\n(`156db38b8d16`) covered all 116 API cases with the same per-sample L2\nclearing. Across three runs per version, geometric-mean GPU time changed\nby +0.53% on GB300 and -0.18% on H100. The 6 MiB Blackwell scale cases\nshowed bimodal timing in separate processes; alternating the old and new\ndispatch on identical tensors reproduced identical compiled kernels and\nlaunch arguments, with no median slowdown above 0.47% across eight cases\n(six measurements per version). No reproducible GPU-time regression was\nfound. These measurements exclude CPU allocation and dispatch overhead.\n\nThe tests cover every public layout argument involved here with the\nfinite parameter matrix below. Both conversion directions are checked\nagainst CPU references; this does not claim exhaustive coverage of\narbitrary integer sizes.\n\n| Layout | Argument coverage |\n|---|---|\n| `StridedLayout` | Every physical axis of rank-2, rank-3, and rank-4\ntensors, with positive/negative equivalence checked separately on CPU;\nleading-axis FP4 packing uses the fallback path. |\n| `BlackwellMXValueLayout`, `BlackwellMXScaleLayout` | No constructor\narguments. |\n| `BlackwellMX4ValueShuffledLayout` | Cartesian product of\n`block_k={64,128,192,256}` and `block_n={32,48,128,256}`, with guarded\nstrided output. |\n| `BlackwellActMXScaleLayout` | `ragged_metadata=None`, mixed\nempty/nonempty slices, all-empty slices, and zero slices with zero or\npositive logical capacity; both source and destination majors and their\nindex aliases. |\n| `HopperMXScaleLayout` | Both physical matrix axes; positive/negative\nequivalence checked separately on CPU; `num_warps={1,2,4,8,16,32}`;\nallocated and guarded preallocated outputs. Also checks below/at/above\nthe 1-MiB dispatch boundary. |\n| `HopperMXValueLayout` | Existing tests cover `mx_axis={-2,-1}` and\n`mma_version={2,3}`. |\n\n```bash\npython -m pytest -s --tb=short python/triton_kernels/tests/test_tensor_details/test_layout_blackwell.py\npython -m pytest -s --tb=short python/triton_kernels/tests/test_tensor_details/test_layout_hopper.p